WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0006: the maximum profit from a stock,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.100
Two parts: first long trades only,
then short trades too.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:13.933
An array prices holds the stock's price on each day.
One trade is allowed.

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:19.633
Part A: buy on one day,
sell on a later day. A long trade.

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:24.633
The page's prices: 5, 8, 3, 9, 2, 6,
one price per day.

6
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:29.733
Buy on day i, sell on day j > i.
The profit: prices[j] − prices[i].

7
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:34.767
The direct way checks every pair of days:
15 pairs here, O(n²) time in general.

8
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:40.367
The idea: when selling on day j, the best buy
is at the lowest price up to that day.

9
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:45.167
So it is enough to remember the minimum so far
and scan the array once.

10
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:51.467
This is the page's box, on the same prices.
The blue line is the minimum so far.

11
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:55.700
Day 0: the price is 5. There is no earlier day,
so 5 is the minimum.

12
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.933
Day 1: 8 − 5 = 3.
The best long so far earns 3.

13
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:04.467
Day 2: 3 − 5 is negative, no profit.
But 3 is the new minimum.

14
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:09.133
Day 3: 9 − 3 = 6. A new best long:
buy on day 2, sell on day 3.

15
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:12.667
Day 4: 2 − 3 is negative.
The new minimum is 2.

16
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:17.333
Day 5: 6 − 2 = 4, less than 6.
The answer: profit 6, days 2 and 3.

17
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:21.800
Now let's write it in Python, line by line.

18
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:27.933
max_long_fast takes the list of prices and returns
the profit, the buy day and the sell day.

19
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:33.967
Line 2: no trade yet.
Profit 0, and no days.

20
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:41.467
Lines 3–4: an empty list returns
"no trade" right away.

21
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.100
Line 5: the minimum so far starts
at the first day's price, day 0.

22
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.900
Line 6: go over the days in order.
i is the day, p its price.

23
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.600
Lines 7–8: sell today, bought at the minimum.
A larger profit is saved with both days.

24
00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:10.767
Lines 9–10: update the minimum only after the check,
so we never buy and sell on the same day.

25
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.000
Line 11: return the best trade.

26
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.167
Let's run the code on the example,
with the page's box stepping along.

27
00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:25.567
Lines 2–5: no trade yet,
and the minimum is 5, from day 0.

28
00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:31.100
Day 0: 5 − 5 = 0, not more than 0.
The minimum stays 5.

29
00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:36.633
Day 1: 8 − 5 = 3. Line 8 saves profit 3,
buy on day 0, sell on day 1.

30
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:42.167
Day 2: 3 − 5 is negative, and line 10
moves the minimum to 3, on day 2.

31
00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:47.700
Day 3: 9 − 3 = 6, more than 3.
The new trade: days 2 and 3.

32
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.233
Day 4: 2 − 3 is negative.
The minimum drops to 2, on day 4.

33
00:02:53.233 --> 00:02:58.767
Day 5: 6 − 2 = 4, less than 6.
Nothing changes.

34
00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:03.500
The loop is done. The answer:
profit 6, buy on day 2, sell on day 3.

35
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.700
Why it works: for each sell day j, the best buy
is at the minimum of the days before it.

36
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:15.500
The loop checks exactly that value for every day,
so its maximum is the maximum over all pairs.

37
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.467
The trap: the highest price minus
the lowest price of the whole array.

38
00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:26.967
For 9, 1 the difference is 8, but the buy must come
before the sale: no long makes a profit.

39
00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:33.267
The cost: one pass, O(n) time, and O(1) memory,
instead of O(n²) for all pairs.

40
00:03:34.065 --> 00:03:37.265
Part B: the twist. Short selling is allowed too.

41
00:03:38.765 --> 00:03:43.165
A short: sell on day i,
buy it back on a later day j.

42
00:03:43.165 --> 00:03:47.165
The short's profit: prices[i] − prices[j].
It earns when the price falls.

43
00:03:47.165 --> 00:03:52.199
In the example, the best long earns 6,
and the best short earns 7.

44
00:03:52.199 --> 00:03:55.699
So the answer: short, profit 7, days 3 and 4.

45
00:03:56.299 --> 00:04:00.599
The idea: a short's profit is minus
the long's profit on the same days.

46
00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:05.999
So for the short we remember the maximum so far,
and both run in the same loop.

47
00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:11.965
In the box: the blue line is the minimum so far,
the orange one the maximum so far.

48
00:04:11.965 --> 00:04:15.099
Day 0: the minimum and the maximum are 5.

49
00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:18.699
Day 1: long 8 − 5 = 3.
The maximum rises to 8.

50
00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:22.299
Day 2: short 8 − 3 = 5.
The minimum drops to 3.

51
00:04:22.299 --> 00:04:25.899
Day 3: long 9 − 3 = 6.
The maximum rises to 9.

52
00:04:25.899 --> 00:04:29.499
Day 4: short 9 − 2 = 7.
The minimum drops to 2.

53
00:04:29.499 --> 00:04:34.532
Day 5: long 4, short 3, no improvement.
The short, 7, beats the long, 6.

54
00:04:35.332 --> 00:04:38.732
In Python it is the same loop, with two candidates.

55
00:04:40.699 --> 00:04:45.199
max_profit_fast returns the profit, the trade's type,
and its opening and closing days.

56
00:04:48.665 --> 00:04:53.165
Lines 2–3: a trade needs two days.
With fewer than two prices there is none.

57
00:04:57.565 --> 00:05:02.065
Lines 5–6: the minimum and maximum so far,
both start at day 0's price.

58
00:05:08.999 --> 00:05:13.499
Lines 8–9: two candidates, long and short,
each with profit 0 and no days.

59
00:05:15.832 --> 00:05:18.732
Line 11: one loop over all the days.

60
00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:29.099
Lines 12–14: the long check, as in part A,
against the minimum of the earlier days.

61
00:05:35.432 --> 00:05:39.932
Lines 16–18: the mirror image for the short:
the maximum so far minus today's price.

62
00:05:45.965 --> 00:05:50.465
Lines 20–23: only after both checks
are the minimum and maximum updated.

63
00:05:56.632 --> 00:06:01.032
Lines 25–26: if the short beats the long,
return it.

64
00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:12.765
Lines 27–29: otherwise the long, if it earns.
If not, there is no trade.

65
00:06:13.465 --> 00:06:17.432
Let's run it on the example, day by day,
beside the page's box.

66
00:06:17.432 --> 00:06:22.465
Lines 2–9: the minimum and maximum are 5,
and both candidates have profit 0.

67
00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:28.632
Day 0: no profit either way.

68
00:06:28.632 --> 00:06:34.865
Day 1: long 8 − 5 = 3, and line 23
raises the maximum to 8.

69
00:06:34.865 --> 00:06:40.399
Day 2: short 8 − 3 = 5, and line 21
lowers the minimum to 3.

70
00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:45.932
Day 3: long 9 − 3 = 6,
and the maximum rises to 9.

71
00:06:45.932 --> 00:06:51.465
Day 4: short 9 − 2 = 7,
and the minimum drops to 2.

72
00:06:51.465 --> 00:06:56.932
Day 5: long 4, short 3, no improvement.

73
00:06:56.932 --> 00:07:01.465
The loop is done: the best long is 6,
the best short 7.

74
00:07:01.465 --> 00:07:06.199
Line 25: 7 is more than 6, so it returns
short, profit 7, days 3 and 4.

75
00:07:06.999 --> 00:07:12.032
The cost: still one pass, O(n) time,
and constant extra memory, O(1).

76
00:07:12.032 --> 00:07:17.332
For a million days: about 500 billion pairs,
against only a million steps.

77
00:07:18.132 --> 00:07:23.032
A trap: a tie between long and short.
For 1, 4, 1: long 3 and short 3.

78
00:07:23.032 --> 00:07:28.532
The strict comparison on line 25 returns the long.
Any pair with the maximum profit is correct.

79
00:07:29.327 --> 00:07:34.860
In short: the minimum so far gives the long,
and the maximum so far gives the short.

80
00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.894
Try your own prices in the box
on the page of question 0006.
